<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The structure converts site-of-care ma=
nagement from a contracting tactic into a jointly owned operating model. IBX =
gains a mechanism to steer appropriate procedures toward lower-cost facilitie=
s; Penn accepts lower reimbursement per case but can retain referrals, physic=
ian alignment and ownership income rather than losing volume to independent A=
SCs.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The trade-off is pressure on hospital-=
outpatient fixed-cost absorption. If successful, the model could encourage ot=
her regional Blues plans and health systems to co-invest rather than continue=
 negotiating over HOPD rates after capacity has already been built.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Ownership economics, payer steering me=
chanisms, physician participation and whether new ASC volume represents marke=
t growth or cannibalization of Penn hospital departments.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-4" name=3D"story-4" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>4. Three national payers back a s=
hared credentialing utility</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">UnitedHealthcare, Cigna Healthcare and=
 Centene became founding partners in CertifyOS=E2=80=99s SharedCred program. =
The model creates one governed primary-source verification packet and coordin=
ated recredentialing event while allowing each plan to retain its own standar=
ds, committee review and network decisions. CertifyOS says more than 1.4 mill=
ion providers are initially in scope; that scale figure is vendor-reported, a=
nd the service is scheduled to come online this fall. (<a href=3D"https://www=
.certifyos.com/blogs/why-we-built-sharedcred" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-dec=
oration:underline;">CertifyOS, =E2=80=9CWhy We Built SharedCred,=E2=80=9D Sep=
tember 2026</a>; <a href=3D"https://www.certifyos.com/" style=3D"color:#1a5e9=
a;text-decoration:underline;">CertifyOS, =E2=80=9CIntroducing SharedCred,=E2=
=80=9D accessed September 28, 2026</a>). (<a href=3D"https://www.certifyos.co=
m/blogs/why-we-built-sharedcred?utm_source=3Dopenai" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;t=
ext-decoration:underline;">certifyos.com</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Participation by three large carriers =
gives shared credentialing a credible path from point solution to industry ut=
ility. Successful execution could shorten network onboarding, reduce repeated=
 provider outreach and improve the upstream data feeding directories, claims =
edits and prior authorization.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">It also creates displacement risk for =
standalone credential-verification workflows. The strategic asset becomes the=
 governed provider-data network and audit trail=E2=80=94not the act of repeat=
ing primary-source verification for each payer.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Additional payer participation, measur=
ed onboarding-time improvements and whether provider groups accept a new cent=
ralized data steward alongside existing CAQH workflows.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-5" name=3D"story-5" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>5. Oracle extends its agent strat=
egy into the full revenue cycle</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Last week=E2=80=99s thesis was that Or=
acle=E2=80=99s agentic EHR expanded product ambition without resolving implem=
entation risk. On September 23, Oracle added planned AI capabilities spanning=
 prior authorization, documentation integrity, charge capture, professional c=
oding and appeal-packet creation, with general availability expected over the=
 coming months. Oracle also intends to connect reimbursement activity with it=
s Fusion financial applications. (<a href=3D"https://www.oracle.com/news/anno=
uncement/oracle-health-advances-revenue-cycle-management-with-ai-2026-09-23/"=
 style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">Oracle, =E2=80=9COracle H=
ealth Advances Revenue Cycle Management with AI,=E2=80=9D September 23, 2026<=
/a>). (<a href=3D"https://www.oracle.com/news/announcement/oracle-health-adva=
nces-revenue-cycle-management-with-ai-2026-09-23/?utm_source=3Dopenai" style=
=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">oracle.com</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Oracle is no longer positioning AI sol=
ely as a clinical-workflow layer; it is targeting the denial-prevention and c=
ash-conversion economics that fund RCM purchases. Native access to EHR, patie=
nt-accounting and enterprise-finance data could reduce integration burdens an=
d pressure point vendors.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The thesis remains execution-dependent=
: announced capabilities are not yet evidence of production accuracy, payer c=
onnectivity or improved collections.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Named production customers, general-av=
ailability dates and measurable effects on authorization turnaround, denial r=
ates, coding productivity and days in accounts receivable.</p>
